= Expert Strategy for Wave 2
== Campaign Strategy on Legendary
=== The First 50 Turns (Survival)
+ #strong[Legendary AI cheats] --- AI gets public order and morale
  bonuses. Plan for it.
+ #strong[No battle saves] --- every battle matters. Auto-resolve is
  more punishing.
+ #strong[Aggressive expansion in the first 20 turns] --- cripple your
  nearest rival before they grow
+ #strong[Accept that you will lose settlements] --- plan to retake, not
  to hold everything

=== Mod-Overhauled Systems
==== Diplomacy Overhaul
- The overhaul mod changes negotiation to a offer-demand system
- You must offer something of value for every request
- Trust is a resource --- breaking treaties permanently damages
  relationships
- War declarations require casus belli or heavy diplomatic penalties

==== Economy Overhaul
- Population mechanics: larger cities drain food, generate wealth but
  require more public order
- Tax rate affects population growth --- high taxes slow growth
- Trade route banditry: invest in road infrastructure to protect trade

==== Character Overhaul
- Generals have loyalty factions --- they may rebel with their entire
  army
- Court politics matter: promote talented generals or risk defection
- Family tree management is critical --- succession crises can end
  campaigns

=== Endgame Crisis Events
- #strong[Horde armies] spawn from unguarded borders --- keep reserve
  forces
- #strong[Economic collapses] trigger when corruption reaches thresholds
  --- reform down
- #strong[Dilemmas] force choices between short-term gain and long-term
  stability
- #strong[Rival kingdoms] coordinate against the player --- divide and
  conquer

=== Battle Tactics for Legendary
- #strong[Never autoresolve] against equal or greater forces --- the
  calculator is ruthless
- #strong[General sniping] is harder (AI protects generals better) ---
  use cavalry traps
- #strong[Morale management] is key --- your units will break faster
  without your general nearby
- #strong[Terrain is your only advantage] --- always fight on favorable
  ground
- #strong[Withdraw from unwinnable battles] --- preserving armies is
  preserving campaign viability

== Advanced Campaign Mastery (Phase 3)
=== Emperor Mechanics
- Once you declare yourself #strong[Emperor] (or another faction does),
  the game shifts. You gain access to #strong[Imperial Seals] ---
  powerful ancillaries that grant faction-wide bonuses.
- Seals are awarded for achieving prestige milestones. Prioritize
  prestige generation once you control 60+ territories.
- The #strong[Three Kingdoms] phase triggers when three factions reach
  King or Emperor status. At this point, the two rival emperors become
  permanent enemies.

=== Vassals vs Direct Conquest
- #strong[Vassals] are useful buffers --- they guard borders and
  contribute armies --- but they generate less income than directly
  owned territories.
- Use vassals for regions with different culture (e.g., Nanman
  territories) where public order would be a nightmare.
- Direct conquest is better for wealthy commanderies with your culture.
  Vassalize the poor ones.
- You can #strong[integrate] vassals later, but it takes time and
  satisfaction penalties. Plan your integration order.

=== Satisfaction & Corruption at Scale
- At large empire size (10+ commanderies), #strong[corruption] eats
  30-50% of your income if unchecked. Prioritize corruption-reducing
  reforms and buildings.
- #strong[Satisfaction management] becomes a full-time job. Every turn,
  check your character list --- anyone below 50 satisfaction needs an
  immediate promotion, ancillary, or marriage.
- Use #strong[Administrators] in every commandery --- they reduce
  corruption and increase income enough to justify their salary.
- The #strong[Prime Minister] position is critical at this stage. Assign
  your highest-ranking character with "+satisfaction to all" traits.

=== Character Skill Trees
- By Phase 3, your core generals should be rank 6+. Plan their skill
  trees:
  - #strong[Commanders:] Focus on "+satisfaction to all" and army
    movement range.
  - #strong[Vanguards:] Damage and charge bonuses. They are your field
    generals.
  - #strong[Champions:] Personal dueling skills and unit buffs.
  - #strong[Strategists:] Ranged damage, formation abilities, and
    cunning for spy networks.
  - #strong[Sentinels:] Defense, garrison bonuses, and administrator
    skills.
- Do not waste skill points on redundant abilities. Specialize each
  general for their role.

=== Timing Major Wars
- Do not declare yourself Emperor until you have 3+ elite armies ready.
  The diplomatic penalty with everyone else is severe --- expect
  coalitions to form against you.
- Break alliances and coalitions #strong[before] declaring war. The
  trust penalty is worth avoiding the "ally joined enemy" problem.
- Time your wars when your target is already fighting someone else. Let
  them exhaust their armies, then strike.

=== Training Exercises
+ Play a full campaign where you deliberately become Emperor as late as
  possible while still dominating the map.
+ Run a "tall" campaign (few high-quality commanderies) vs a "wide"
  campaign (rapid expansion).
+ Master the #strong[Yellow Turban] or #strong[Nanman] factions for
  completely different playstyles.

#strong[Success criteria:] Reliably win on Hard/Very Hard. Understand
#emph[why] the AI makes certain diplomatic moves.

== Battle Mastery & Micro (Phase 4)
=== Focus Areas
- #strong[Terrain usage:] Forests block line of sight and reduce missile
  damage. Hills give morale and damage bonuses. Rivers slow movement and
  exhaust units. Choke points negate numerical disadvantages.
- #strong[Flanking:] A unit attacking an enemy from the side or rear
  deals massive morale damage. One flanking cavalry charge can route an
  entire unit.
- #strong[Cycle charging:] Charge, pull back, charge again. Cavalry that
  stays in melee loses its charge bonus. Micro your cavalry for maximum
  impact.
- #strong[Hero duels (Romance mode):] Duels can swing battles instantly.
  Send your champion against enemy generals but pull them back when
  outmatched. In Records mode, general bodyguards are powerful but not
  invincible --- use them as heavy cavalry.
- #strong[Strategist abilities:] Fire arrows, smoke screens, and night
  battles are force multipliers. Use them.
- #strong[Army stances:] March (faster but cannot retreat), Ambush
  (invisible until attack), Raiding (sustain without supply), and Forced
  March (extra range but exhausted in battle). Use the right stance for
  the situation.

=== Training Method
+ Replay the same historical battle (e.g., Guandu-style scenarios)
  multiple times with different army compositions.
+ Practice fighting 1.5x-2x larger armies on the battle map.
+ Watch high-level campaign battle footage and pause to analyze
  decisions.

== Master Level --- Legendary & Optimisation (Phase 5)
=== Legendary Difficulty Adjustments
- #strong[No battle saves] --- every battle matters. Auto-resolve is
  more punishing than manual battle.
- #strong[AI morale bonuses] --- enemy units take longer to break. Plan
  for extended fights.
- #strong[AI public order cheats] --- the AI expands faster without
  rebellion. Do not try to out-expand them --- out-smart them.
- #strong[Minimal reloading] --- embrace mistakes. Legendary is about
  adaptation, not perfection.

=== Min-Maxing Your Empire
- #strong[Character traits:] Recruit and promote characters with
  "+satisfaction," "-corruption," and "+commerce" traits. Remove or
  demote characters with negative traits.
- #strong[Ancillaries:] Every character should have a full ancillary
  loadout. The bonuses compound. Trade for ancillaries in diplomacy if
  needed.
- #strong[Court synergy:] Certain combinations of court positions +
  character traits create powerful synergies. For example, a Prime
  Minister with "+2 satisfaction to all" plus an Heir with "-10%
  corruption" nearly solves two problems at once.
- #strong[Army specialisation:] By late game, build specialized armies
  --- one for siege, one for field battles, one for rapid response.
  Don't make every army a generalist.

=== Final Challenges
+ Win a Legendary campaign as a hard starting faction (Kong Rong, Liu
  Biao, or a Yellow Turban).
+ Unify China before turn 100 on Very Hard/Legendary.
+ Play a full campaign with self-imposed restrictions (no spies, no
  vassals, only one army type, etc.).

